<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">Section 6.6 Exercises</heading>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, calculate the center of mass for the collection of masses given.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="254" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$m_{1} = 2$$ at $$x_{1} = 1$$ and $$m_{2} = 4$$ at $$x_{2} = 2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="255" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$m_{1} = 1$$ at $$x_{1} = -1$$ and $$m_{2} = 3$$ at $$x_{2} = 2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$5 4 5 4$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="256" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$m = 3$$ at $$x = 0,1,2,6$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="257" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Unit masses at $$(x,y) = (1,0),(0,1),(1,1)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 2 3 , 2 3 ) ( 2 3 , 2 3 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="258" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$m_{1} = 1$$ at $$(1,0)$$ and $$m_{2} = 4$$ at $$(0,1)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="259" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$m_{1} = 1$$ at $$(1,0)$$ and $$m_{2} = 3$$ at $$(2,2)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 7 4 , 3 2 ) ( 7 4 , 3 2 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, compute the center of mass $$\overset{-}{x}.$$</paragraph>
    <exercise number="260" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = 1$$ for $$x \in (-1,3)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="261" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = x^{2}$$ for $$x \in (0,L)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$3 L 4 3 L 4$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="262" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = 1$$ for $$x \in (0,1)$$ and $$\rho = 2$$ for $$x \in (1,2)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="263" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = \sin x$$ for $$x \in (0,\pi)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi 2 \pi 2$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="264" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = \cos x$$ for $$x \in \left( {0,\frac{\pi}{2}} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="265" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = e^{x}$$ for $$x \in \left( {0,2} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$e 2 + 1 e 2 - 1 e 2 + 1 e 2 - 1$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="266" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = x^{3} + xe^{-x}$$ for $$x \in (0,1)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="267" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = x \sin x$$ for $$x \in (0,\pi)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi 2 - 4 \pi \pi 2 - 4 \pi$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="268" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = \sqrt{x}$$ for $$x \in \left( {1,4} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="269" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = \ln x$$ for $$x \in \left( {1,e} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$1 4 ( 1 + e 2 ) 1 4 ( 1 + e 2 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, compute the center of mass $$\left( {\overset{-}{x},\overset{-}{y}} \right).$$ Use symmetry to help locate the center of mass whenever possible.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="270" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = 7$$ in the square $$0 \leq x \leq 1,$$ $$0 \leq y \leq 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="271" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = 3$$ in the triangle with vertices $$(0,0),$$ $$(a,0),$$ and $$(0,b)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( a 3 , b 3 ) ( a 3 , b 3 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="272" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\rho = 2$$ for the region bounded by $$y = \cos (x),$$ $$y = -\cos (x),$$ $$x = - \frac{\pi}{2},$$ and $$x = \frac{\pi}{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use a calculator to draw the region, then compute the center of mass $$\left( {\overset{-}{x},\overset{-}{y}} \right).$$ Use symmetry to help locate the center of mass whenever possible.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="273" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] The region bounded by $$y = \cos (2x),$$ $$x = - \frac{\pi}{4},$$ and $$x = \frac{\pi}{4}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 0 , \pi 8 ) ( 0 , \pi 8 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="274" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] The region between $$y = 2x^{2},$$ $$y = 0,$$ $$x = 0,$$ and $$x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="275" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] The region between $$y = \frac{5}{4}x^{2}$$ and $$y = 5$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 0 , 3 ) ( 0 , 3 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="276" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Region between $$y = \sqrt{x},$$ $$y = \ln (x),$$ $$x = 1,$$ and $$x = 4$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="277" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] The region bounded by $$y = 0,$$ $$\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 0 , 4 \pi ) ( 0 , 4 \pi )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="278" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] The region bounded by $$y = 0,$$ $$x = 0,$$ and $$\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="279" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] The region bounded by $$y = x^{2}$$ and $$y = x^{4}$$ in the first quadrant</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 5 8 , 1 3 ) ( 5 8 , 1 3 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use the theorem of Pappus to determine the volume of the shape.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="280" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Rotating $$y = mx$$ around the $$x$$ -axis between $$x = 0$$ and $$x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="281" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Rotating $$y = mx$$ around the $$y$$ -axis between $$x = 0$$ and $$x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$2 m \pi 3 2 m \pi 3$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="282" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A general cone created by rotating a triangle with vertices $$(0,0),$$ $$(a,0),$$ and $$(0,b)$$ around the $$y$$ -axis. Does your answer agree with the volume of a cone?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="283" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A general cylinder created by rotating a rectangle with vertices $$(0,0),$$ $$(a,0),(0,b),$$ and $$(a,b)$$ around the $$y$$ -axis. Does your answer agree with the volume of a cylinder?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi a 2 b \pi a 2 b$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="284" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A sphere created by rotating a semicircle with radius $$a$$ around the $$y$$ -axis. Does your answer agree with the volume of a sphere?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use a calculator to draw the region enclosed by the curve. Find the area $$M$$ and the centroid $$\left( {\overset{-}{x},\overset{-}{y}} \right)$$ for the given shapes. Use symmetry to help locate the center of mass whenever possible.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="285" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Quarter-circle: $$y = \sqrt{1 - x^{2}},$$ $$y = 0,$$ and $$x = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 4 3 \pi , 4 3 \pi ) ( 4 3 \pi , 4 3 \pi )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="286" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Triangle: $$y = x,$$ $$y = 2 - x,$$ and $$y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="287" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Lens: $$y = x^{2}$$ and $$y = x$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 1 2 , 2 5 ) ( 1 2 , 2 5 )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="288" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Ring: $$y^{2} + x^{2} = 1$$ and $$y^{2} + x^{2} = 4$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="289" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Half-ring: $$y^{2} + x^{2} = 1,$$ $$y^{2} + x^{2} = 4,$$ and $$y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$( 0 , 28 9 \pi ) ( 0 , 28 9 \pi )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="290" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the generalized center of mass in the sliver between $$y = x^{a}$$ and $$y = x^{b}$$ with $$a &gt; b.$$ Then, use the Pappus theorem to find the volume of the solid generated when revolving around the y-axis.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="291" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the generalized center of mass between $$y = a^{2} - x^{2},$$ $$x = 0,$$ and $$y = 0.$$ Then, use the Pappus theorem to find the volume of the solid generated when revolving around the y-axis.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Center of mass: $$(3a8,2a25),(3a8,2a25),$$ volume: $$\pi a42\pi a42$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="292" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the generalized center of mass between $$y = b \sin (ax),$$ $$x = 0,$$ and $$x = \frac{\pi}{a}.$$ Then, use the Pappus theorem to find the volume of the solid generated when revolving around the y-axis.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="293" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the theorem of Pappus to find the volume of a torus (pictured here). Assume that a disk of radius $$a$$ is positioned with the left end of the circle at $$x = b,$$ $$b &gt; 0,$$ and is rotated around the y-axis.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Volume: $$2\pi 2a2(b+a)2\pi 2a2(b+a)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="294" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the center of mass $$\left( {\overset{-}{x},\overset{-}{y}} \right)$$ for a thin wire along the semicircle $$y = \sqrt{1 - x^{2}}$$ with unit mass. (Hint: Use the theorem of Pappus.)</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
